  "description": "A tiny lesson with a big payoff: How Sound Becomes Signal.",
  "is_free": true,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Sound is vibrating air. Your ear catches those vibrations and turns them into electrical brain signals.",
      "visual": "🌊"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Sound waves funnel through the outer ear, vibrate the eardrum, and pass through three tiny bones in the middle ear.",
      "visual": "🦴"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The cochlea in the inner ear is a fluid-filled spiral lined with 15,000 hair cells that bend with each vibration.",
      "visual": "🐚"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Each position along the cochlea responds to a different frequency. High notes at the base, low notes at the tip.",
      "visual": "🎹"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Hair cells don't regenerate in humans. Loud noise destroys them permanently, causing irreversible hearing loss.",
      "visual": "🔊"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Your 15,000 hair cells are irreplaceable. Protect them or lose them forever.",
      "visual": "🛡️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "Can damaged hair cells in the human cochlea regenerate?",
    "options": [
      "No, the damage is permanent",
      "Yes, they regenerate every month",
      "Only during childhood"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
